Fluctuating chemically active droplets
Guido L. A. Kusters,1 Noah Ziethen,2 Michael E. Cates,2 and David Zwicker1
1Max Planck Institute for Dynamics and Self-Organization, Go ̈ttingen, Germany 2DAMTP, Centre for Mathematical Sciences, University of Cambridge, Cambridge CB3 0WA, United Kingdom
We study size fluctuations of chemically active droplets in the regime of weak reactions and strong phase separation. By combining a thin-interface model for the deterministic droplet growth with stochastic noise based on fluctuation-dissipation arguments, we report excellent quantitative agreement between our theory and numerical field simulations. Finally, we use our model to infer the effective non-equilibrium potential that governs droplet growth, and show that active reactions that enable size control suppress nucleation.
Chemically active droplets combine phase separation and non-equilibrium reactions, with particular relevance to biology [1–12] and engineered synthetic systems [13– 26]. Noise is relevant in these systems because droplets are typically small and soft [27], which results in significant size fluctuations, but also for the study of nucleation. While both of these aspects have been studied extensively in passive droplets [28–35], and in special cases for active droplets [36–47], there currently exists no comprehensive theory for fluctuating chemically active droplets. In this Letter, we study fluctuating chemically active droplets in the regime of weak reactions and strong phase separation. In this case, the interface dynamics is governed by local diffusive fluxes, as in Model B [48]. However, in contrast to Model B, reactions modify those fluxes and break material conservation. In particular, sustained energy input in chemically active droplets can lead to non-equilibrium stationary states with finite droplet sizes. We address fluctuations in this regime by deriving an effective Langevin equation for the droplet radius that combines the full deterministic dynamics with noise derived in the passive limit. Our theory predicts droplet size fluctuations in excellent quantitative agreement with numerical field simulations, and describes when active reactions suppress nucleation. We consider an incompressible, binary fluid, comprising components A and B, as a minimal model for chemically active droplets. The system’s state is specified by the volume fraction fieldφ(⃗r) of component A, whereas component B occupies the fraction 1−φ(⃗r). The evolution follows from the continuity equation,
