In Fig.1(d), we plot only those data points that have reached the plateau of the autocorrelation function within the simulation time, which we test for by dividing the range 5×10 4 τ≤∆t≤10 5 τinto 5 temporal blocks and evaluating the mean of the autocorrelation function in each. For a data point to be categorized as plateau-reaching, there must be no systematic trend between the block means (no more than 20% of subsequent blocks can have increasing or decreasing means), and the relative trend (difference in mean between last and first block divided by the average of block means) and spread (standard deviation of block means divided by average of block means) must be below the threshold 0.1.
